# SrTiO3 (Pm-3m) with METAGGA = LAK and NBANDS = 64: the three VASP runs of
# `crystod --diagram --vasp`
#
#   crystod --diagram --co-left SrTi --co-right O3 --vasp .
#
# Layout
#   BAND/                     the crystal run            Sr Ti O        NELECT 32
#   BAND_sublattice1/         the cation sublattice      Sr Ti Va2-     NELECT  8
#   BAND_sublattice2/         the anion sublattice       Va2+ Va4+ O    NELECT 24
#
# Why this data set is in the test suite
#   Two defects of the engine showed up on this run, and the suite keeps them fixed.
#
#   1. The rigid alignment paired its anchor with the wrong crystal level.  The PROCAR
#      basis has ONE channel per (atom, l), so at R -- where no O state shares the irrep
#      R2- -- the Sr 4s R2- semicore fragment level overlaps its own band R2- #1
#      (-34.07 eV) and the empty Sr 5s-like level R2- #2 (+11.69 eV) both at 1.000, and
#      the Sr 5s and 6s fragment levels overlap both just as much.  The PBE fixture next
#      to this one has the same tie (R2- #2 at +10.26 eV) and got the right level only
#      because its two overlaps came out bit-identical and the first maximum was kept.
#      Here the semicore overlap came out 4e-16 below 1, the empty level won, and the
#      rigid cation-column shift came out at +15.695 eV with a 45.779 eV spread over the
#      four k points (+50.022 eV with --kpoint R alone).  The anchor counterparts are now
#      assigned one-to-one in energy order: +4.257 eV, spread 0.025 eV, and +4.268 eV at
#      R alone, and the PBE fixture keeps its numbers by construction.  The rigid
#      shift is the --vasp-align rigid alignment, the diagnostic line of the default
#      site-resolved one, and the first-pass anchor-window cut of the site-resolved fit:
#      with --kpoint R that cut lost every Sr and Ti anchor and the cation column
#      disappeared from the page.  The default four-k-point site-resolved alignment
#      (Sr +4.154, Ti +14.088, O +6.284 eV) was right before and is unchanged.
#
#   2. The functional.  A meta-GGA OUTCAR names its functional on a "METAGGA = LAK"
#      line and has no "GGA = ..." line, so the report, the page chip and the JSON said
#      "PAW".  They now say LAK.
#
# What was trimmed
#   Real output of three finished VASP 6.5.1 runs (PAW_PBE datasets, METAGGA = LAK,
#   LASPH, ENCUT 550 eV, LORBIT 12, NBANDS 64; a shifted 10x10x10 mesh of 35
#   irreducible points plus a 253-point zero-weight band path).  PROCAR and EIGENVAL
#   were restricted to the four k points the diagram reads -- GM, X, M and R, each at
#   its FIRST occurrence on the band path, which is the block the engine reads -- and
#   their headers rewritten to match.  The complex LORBIT = 12 phase tables are
#   untouched, so the projections, irreps, shares and connector weights are the real
#   ones; the files are gzipped, and the whole fixture is about 77 kB instead of 94 MB.
#
#   The OUTCARs are excerpts, under the same rule as the SrTiO3 and CaF2 fixtures next
#   to this one: the run parameters (including the INCAR echo of METAGGA and the
#   "METAGGA = LAK    functional components" line of the exchange-correlation summary),
#   the Fermi level, the CrystOD Va point-charge table, and the two lines that name
#   each PAW dataset and its valence configuration.  The PAW header tables of a real
#   OUTCAR are a verbatim dump of the POTCAR header and are NOT reproduced here; no
#   POTCAR file is shipped, and nothing from the VASP source tree is.
#
# Fidelity
#   The report this fixture produces is identical, line for line, to the one the
#   untrimmed runs produce, apart from the run paths: the valence-band maximum is at R,
#   which the fixture keeps, and every other number comes from the four special points.
#
# The full runs, the modified VASP that understands the Va species, and the patch
# itself are not part of CrystOD; VASP is proprietary and the patch is available to
# VASP licensees on request.
